1
00:00:00,275 --> 00:00:04,550
This is a computer simulation of fluid flowing past a cylinder.

2
00:00:04,550 --> 00:00:09,138
Notice the swirls peeling off, alternating between the top and the bottom,

3
00:00:09,138 --> 00:00:13,108
one after another, in a perfectly rhythmic pattern.

4
00:00:13,108 --> 00:00:16,121
This pattern is called a Kármán vortex street.

5
00:00:16,121 --> 00:00:19,883
You can see it in the clouds downwind of islands, and it's why a telephone

6
00:00:19,883 --> 00:00:22,342
wire hums in the wind.

7
00:00:22,342 --> 00:00:27,304
Every swirl here, every detail of this flow, comes from a single set of equations,

8
00:00:27,304 --> 00:00:29,467
written down two centuries ago:

9
00:00:29,467 --> 00:00:32,867
the Navier–Stokes equations.

10
00:00:33,383 --> 00:00:37,433
They govern the weather, ocean currents, the blood in your arteries, the air

11
00:00:37,433 --> 00:00:41,783
over an airplane's wing, and the cream swirling in your coffee.

12
00:00:41,783 --> 00:00:44,208
At first glance they look intimidating.

13
00:00:44,208 --> 00:00:47,071
But the core idea is something you already know.

14
00:00:47,071 --> 00:00:51,671
They are nothing more than Newton's second law, F equals m a, applied to every

15
00:00:51,671 --> 00:00:53,950
tiny parcel of fluid.

16
00:00:53,950 --> 00:00:57,337
By the end of this video, we'll have derived every one of these terms from

17
00:00:57,337 --> 00:01:02,000
scratch, and every symbol will feel not just familiar, but inevitable.

18
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:05,862
Along the way, we'll see why these innocent-looking equations hide one of

19
00:01:05,862 --> 00:01:10,475
the great problems in mathematics, with a million-dollar prize attached, and

20
00:01:10,475 --> 00:01:15,300
one that an AI system, in September 2026, claimed to have cracked.

21
00:01:22,067 --> 00:01:24,404
So, how do you even describe a fluid?

22
00:01:24,404 --> 00:01:28,842
A glass of water holds roughly ten to the twenty-five molecules, all jostling

23
00:01:28,842 --> 00:01:32,154
around, colliding billions of times per second.

24
00:01:32,154 --> 00:01:34,642
Tracking each one is hopeless.

25
00:01:34,642 --> 00:01:35,967
So we zoom out.

26
00:01:35,967 --> 00:01:40,354
We pretend the fluid is a smooth, continuous substance, and at every point

27
00:01:40,354 --> 00:01:42,792
in space we ask just one question:

28
00:01:42,792 --> 00:01:46,750
which way, and how fast, is the fluid moving right here?

29
00:01:46,750 --> 00:01:50,650
The answer is a vector, which we'll call u, and color blue throughout this

30
00:01:50,650 --> 00:01:51,650
video.

31
00:01:51,650 --> 00:01:56,267
Its direction is the direction of flow, and its length is the speed.

32
00:01:56,267 --> 00:02:00,917
Do this at every point and you get a velocity field, written u of x and t,

33
00:02:00,917 --> 00:02:03,505
since it depends on position and on time.

34
00:02:03,505 --> 00:02:06,230
In three dimensions it has three components:

35
00:02:06,230 --> 00:02:08,750
u, v, and w.

36
00:02:08,750 --> 00:02:13,000
If you sprinkle tiny specks of dust into the flow, they get carried along

37
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:16,817
by these arrows, tracing out the motion of the fluid.

38
00:02:16,817 --> 00:02:18,304
We'll need one more field:

39
00:02:18,304 --> 00:02:22,154
pressure, a single number at each point, colored in red.

40
00:02:22,154 --> 00:02:26,392
You can think of it as how tightly the fluid is being squeezed at that spot.

41
00:02:26,392 --> 00:02:29,279
Notice that it's lowest in the middle of each swirl.

42
00:02:29,279 --> 00:02:30,342
Hold on to that;

43
00:02:30,342 --> 00:02:32,275
we'll see why soon.

44
00:02:32,275 --> 00:02:36,475
So the unknowns are the three components of velocity, and the pressure:

45
00:02:36,475 --> 00:02:39,738
four unknown functions of space and time.

46
00:02:39,738 --> 00:02:43,775
The Navier–Stokes equations are the rules that determine how these fields

47
00:02:43,775 --> 00:02:44,550
evolve.

48
00:02:46,823 --> 00:02:48,261
Now for the physics.

49
00:02:48,261 --> 00:02:52,986
Follow one tiny blob of fluid, a parcel, as it drifts along.

50
00:02:52,986 --> 00:02:56,986
Picture it as small enough that the velocity is essentially uniform across

51
00:02:56,986 --> 00:03:01,548
it, yet large enough to contain an enormous number of molecules.

52
00:03:01,548 --> 00:03:07,073
Its volume is dV, and if the fluid's density is rho, its mass is rho times

53
00:03:07,073 --> 00:03:07,448
dV.

54
00:03:07,448 --> 00:03:12,148
Newton's second law says mass times acceleration equals the total force on

55
00:03:12,148 --> 00:03:13,240
it.

56
00:03:13,240 --> 00:03:18,265
Since the parcel is tiny, it's more natural to divide both sides by its volume.

57
00:03:18,265 --> 00:03:21,415
On the left, density times acceleration.

58
00:03:21,415 --> 00:03:25,390
On the right, force per unit volume.

59
00:03:25,540 --> 00:03:28,040
So what forces act on a parcel of fluid?

60
00:03:28,040 --> 00:03:29,640
Only three kinds.

61
00:03:29,640 --> 00:03:34,078
First, the surrounding fluid pushes on it, perpendicular to its surface.

62
00:03:34,078 --> 00:03:35,798
That's pressure.

63
00:03:35,798 --> 00:03:40,736
Second, neighboring fluid sliding past drags on it, tangent to its surface.

64
00:03:40,736 --> 00:03:44,273
That's viscosity, a kind of internal friction.

65
00:03:44,273 --> 00:03:47,948
And third, there can be forces acting on the whole body of the parcel from

66
00:03:47,948 --> 00:03:50,607
outside, like gravity.

67
00:03:50,607 --> 00:03:53,269
So the equation we're after has this shape.

68
00:03:53,269 --> 00:03:57,457
Each piece has a clean mathematical form, and we're going to derive every

69
00:03:57,457 --> 00:03:58,594
one of them.

70
00:03:58,594 --> 00:04:02,269
Let's start with the left side, which turns out to be the subtlest:

71
00:04:02,269 --> 00:04:03,432
acceleration.

72
00:04:05,037 --> 00:04:06,187
Here's a puzzle.

73
00:04:06,187 --> 00:04:09,412
Water flows steadily through a pipe that narrows.

74
00:04:09,412 --> 00:04:12,125
'Steady' means the picture never changes:

75
00:04:12,125 --> 00:04:16,512
at any fixed spot, the velocity now is the same as the velocity a minute from

76
00:04:16,512 --> 00:04:17,771
now.

77
00:04:17,771 --> 00:04:21,471
So if you take the time derivative of the velocity field at a fixed point,

78
00:04:21,471 --> 00:04:24,433
partial u, partial t, you get zero.

79
00:04:24,433 --> 00:04:25,937
Everywhere.

80
00:04:25,937 --> 00:04:28,925
And yet, follow a single parcel of water.

81
00:04:28,925 --> 00:04:33,312
As it enters the narrow section, it must speed up, since the same amount of

82
00:04:33,312 --> 00:04:36,662
water per second has to squeeze through a smaller gap.

83
00:04:36,662 --> 00:04:40,537
The parcel is clearly accelerating.

84
00:04:41,679 --> 00:04:42,879
The resolution:

85
00:04:42,879 --> 00:04:45,267
there are two different rates of change here.

86
00:04:45,267 --> 00:04:49,779
How the velocity changes at a fixed location, and how the velocity changes

87
00:04:49,779 --> 00:04:52,454
for a particular parcel as it travels.

88
00:04:52,454 --> 00:04:56,154
Newton's law is about the second one.

89
00:04:56,396 --> 00:04:57,658
Let's compute it.

90
00:04:57,658 --> 00:05:02,108
Say the parcel's position at time t is x of t, y of t.

91
00:05:02,108 --> 00:05:06,446
Its velocity is just the velocity field evaluated wherever the parcel happens

92
00:05:06,446 --> 00:05:07,704
to be.

93
00:05:07,704 --> 00:05:09,929
Focus on the horizontal component, u.

94
00:05:09,929 --> 00:05:13,267
As time passes, it changes for two reasons.

95
00:05:13,267 --> 00:05:16,404
Time is ticking, so the field itself may change.

96
00:05:16,404 --> 00:05:20,517
And the parcel moves, so it samples the field at new locations.

97
00:05:20,517 --> 00:05:23,137
The chain rule accounts for both.

98
00:05:23,137 --> 00:05:25,137
Here's the key physical input:

99
00:05:25,137 --> 00:05:27,150
the parcel moves with the flow.

100
00:05:27,150 --> 00:05:33,312
So its rate of change of x is just u, and its rate of change of y is v.

101
00:05:35,479 --> 00:05:39,129
Pull the velocity out, and what's left is an operator:

102
00:05:39,129 --> 00:05:43,254
u times partial partial x, plus v times partial partial y.

103
00:05:43,254 --> 00:05:47,679
That's the derivative in the direction of the flow, scaled by the speed, which

104
00:05:47,679 --> 00:05:50,771
we write compactly as u dot del.

105
00:05:50,771 --> 00:05:55,421
The vertical component works exactly the same way, so in vector form:

106
00:05:55,421 --> 00:06:00,421
the acceleration of a parcel is partial u partial t, plus u dot del, u.

107
00:06:00,421 --> 00:06:04,496
This combination is called the material derivative, written with a capital

108
00:06:04,496 --> 00:06:05,779
D.

109
00:06:05,779 --> 00:06:08,917
The first term is the change you'd see sitting still.

110
00:06:08,917 --> 00:06:12,679
The second is the change that comes purely from moving to a place where the

111
00:06:12,679 --> 00:06:14,204
flow is different.

112
00:06:14,204 --> 00:06:17,104
It's called advection.

113
00:06:17,237 --> 00:06:19,725
Here's a nice way to see that second term.

114
00:06:19,725 --> 00:06:22,562
Take a fluid spinning steadily in a circle.

115
00:06:22,562 --> 00:06:26,837
Nothing changes in time, so partial u partial t is zero.

116
00:06:26,837 --> 00:06:31,312
But take the velocity at one point, follow the flow a small step, and compare

117
00:06:31,312 --> 00:06:32,975
with the velocity there.

118
00:06:32,975 --> 00:06:35,629
The difference points toward the center.

119
00:06:35,629 --> 00:06:39,354
So u dot del u is the centripetal acceleration:

120
00:06:39,354 --> 00:06:44,242
the speed squared over the radius, pointing inward, exactly what any object

121
00:06:44,242 --> 00:06:47,454
moving in a circle must have.

122
00:06:47,696 --> 00:06:50,846
Finally, notice that u appears twice in this term:

123
00:06:50,846 --> 00:06:53,221
the flow is carrying itself along.

124
00:06:53,221 --> 00:06:56,496
Double the velocity, and this term quadruples.

125
00:06:56,496 --> 00:07:00,596
This makes the equation nonlinear, and nearly everything that makes fluids

126
00:07:00,596 --> 00:07:05,271
rich, chaotic, and mathematically difficult traces back to this one term.

127
00:07:07,426 --> 00:07:10,013
Now the forces, starting with pressure.

128
00:07:10,013 --> 00:07:14,513
Pressure pushes on any surface, perpendicular to it, with a force equal to

129
00:07:14,513 --> 00:07:16,809
the pressure times the area.

130
00:07:16,809 --> 00:07:20,884
If the pressure is the same on all sides of our parcel, the pushes cancel

131
00:07:20,884 --> 00:07:22,121
perfectly.

132
00:07:22,121 --> 00:07:26,746
A uniform pressure, no matter how large, produces no net force at all.

133
00:07:26,746 --> 00:07:30,409
What matters is how pressure varies.

134
00:07:30,534 --> 00:07:33,471
So suppose the pressure varies from left to right.

135
00:07:33,471 --> 00:07:36,559
Take a box of width dx and face area A.

136
00:07:36,559 --> 00:07:40,934
The left face feels the pressure at x, pushing rightward with force p of x

137
00:07:40,934 --> 00:07:41,884
times A.

138
00:07:41,884 --> 00:07:46,609
The right face feels p at x plus dx, pushing leftward.

139
00:07:46,609 --> 00:07:48,509
The net force is the difference.

140
00:07:48,509 --> 00:07:54,121
For a tiny box, p of x plus dx minus p of x is just the derivative of p times

141
00:07:54,121 --> 00:07:55,134
dx.

142
00:07:55,134 --> 00:07:58,801
And dx times A is the volume of the box.

143
00:07:58,801 --> 00:08:03,601
So the horizontal force per unit volume is minus partial p partial x.

144
00:08:03,601 --> 00:08:08,301
The same argument in the y and z directions gives the other components, and

145
00:08:08,301 --> 00:08:12,251
together they make minus the gradient of p.

146
00:08:12,509 --> 00:08:17,309
The gradient points uphill, toward higher pressure, so the force points downhill.

147
00:08:17,309 --> 00:08:20,942
Fluid gets pushed from high pressure toward low pressure.

148
00:08:20,942 --> 00:08:24,917
It's exactly why wind blows from high-pressure regions toward low-pressure

149
00:08:24,917 --> 00:08:27,067
ones.

150
00:08:27,201 --> 00:08:30,551
And remember those swirls with low pressure in their cores?

151
00:08:30,551 --> 00:08:32,226
Now we can see why.

152
00:08:32,226 --> 00:08:36,626
For fluid to move in a circle, something must supply the inward, centripetal

153
00:08:36,626 --> 00:08:38,788
acceleration we just found.

154
00:08:38,788 --> 00:08:43,709
The only candidate is pressure, so the pressure has to drop toward the center.

155
00:08:43,709 --> 00:08:48,384
Comparing the inward parts, the pressure gradient balances density times speed

156
00:08:48,384 --> 00:08:50,259
squared over radius.

157
00:08:50,259 --> 00:08:54,596
It's why the core of a tornado is a region of very low pressure, and why the

158
00:08:54,596 --> 00:08:57,834
water's surface dips down in the middle of a whirlpool.

159
00:08:59,547 --> 00:09:00,972
Next, viscosity:

160
00:09:00,972 --> 00:09:04,784
the internal friction that makes honey ooze differently from water.

161
00:09:04,784 --> 00:09:09,584
Picture a flow made of thin horizontal layers sliding past each other, faster

162
00:09:09,584 --> 00:09:12,522
at the top, slower at the bottom.

163
00:09:12,522 --> 00:09:15,109
Each layer rubs against its neighbors.

164
00:09:15,109 --> 00:09:19,647
A faster layer above drags a slower one forward, and the slower layer below

165
00:09:19,647 --> 00:09:21,364
holds it back.

166
00:09:21,364 --> 00:09:25,876
For fluids like water and air, this frictional force per unit area, called

167
00:09:25,876 --> 00:09:30,126
the shear stress, is proportional to how quickly the velocity changes from

168
00:09:30,126 --> 00:09:31,926
one layer to the next:

169
00:09:31,926 --> 00:09:33,839
mu times partial u partial y.

170
00:09:33,839 --> 00:09:38,639
The constant mu, in green, is the viscosity.

171
00:09:38,880 --> 00:09:40,668
Now let's derive the force.

172
00:09:40,668 --> 00:09:45,243
Here's a velocity profile, and a slab of fluid of thickness dy.

173
00:09:45,243 --> 00:09:50,068
The layer above pulls it forward with stress tau at y plus dy, times the area

174
00:09:50,068 --> 00:09:50,255
A.

175
00:09:50,255 --> 00:09:54,864
The layer below drags it backward with tau at y, times A.

176
00:09:54,864 --> 00:09:57,664
Once again, the net force comes from a difference.

177
00:09:57,664 --> 00:10:01,226
It's partial tau partial y, times dy times A.

178
00:10:01,226 --> 00:10:05,651
Plug in the stress, and dividing by the volume, the force per unit volume

179
00:10:05,651 --> 00:10:10,047
is mu times the second derivative of u with respect to y.

180
00:10:10,047 --> 00:10:11,622
Notice what this means.

181
00:10:11,622 --> 00:10:16,622
If the velocity profile is a straight line, the second derivative is zero:

182
00:10:16,622 --> 00:10:20,484
the pull from above exactly balances the drag from below.

183
00:10:20,484 --> 00:10:24,805
Viscosity only produces a net force where the profile curves.

184
00:10:24,805 --> 00:10:28,455
When you account for sliding in every direction, and use the fact that the

185
00:10:28,455 --> 00:10:33,530
flow is incompressible, all these second derivatives add up to one operator:

186
00:10:33,530 --> 00:10:37,455
the Laplacian, written del squared.

187
00:10:38,572 --> 00:10:41,459
The Laplacian has a wonderful intuition.

188
00:10:41,459 --> 00:10:45,555
Compare a value at some point to the average of its neighbors.

189
00:10:45,555 --> 00:10:50,843
Using a Taylor expansion on both sides and adding, the first derivatives cancel,

190
00:10:50,843 --> 00:10:55,168
and the second derivative is, up to a constant, the average of the neighbors

191
00:10:55,168 --> 00:10:57,930
minus the value at the point itself.

192
00:10:57,930 --> 00:11:02,130
So the Laplacian is positive where the value sits below its surroundings,

193
00:11:02,130 --> 00:11:06,555
like the bottom of a valley, and negative at a peak.

194
00:11:06,889 --> 00:11:11,314
In two or three dimensions it's the same story, just averaging over neighbors

195
00:11:11,314 --> 00:11:14,114
in every direction.

196
00:11:14,264 --> 00:11:18,351
So viscosity nudges each parcel's velocity toward the average velocity of

197
00:11:18,351 --> 00:11:19,589
its neighbors.

198
00:11:19,589 --> 00:11:23,801
Fast spots get slowed down, slow spots get pulled along.

199
00:11:23,801 --> 00:11:28,589
Peaks get shaved off, valleys get filled in, and the velocity profile smooths

200
00:11:28,589 --> 00:11:30,639
out.

201
00:11:32,330 --> 00:11:37,030
Here's the effect on a full flow, colored by how fast the fluid is spinning.

202
00:11:37,030 --> 00:11:41,293
On the left, a swirling flow with low viscosity, like water.

203
00:11:41,293 --> 00:11:45,768
On the right, the same starting flow with high viscosity, like honey.

204
00:11:45,768 --> 00:11:49,768
The honey version smears out and quickly dies away, while the water keeps

205
00:11:49,768 --> 00:11:51,780
its intricate structure much longer.

206
00:11:57,469 --> 00:12:01,819
We now have one vector equation, three equations in three dimensions.

207
00:12:01,819 --> 00:12:03,694
But there are four unknowns:

208
00:12:03,694 --> 00:12:06,482
three velocity components and the pressure.

209
00:12:06,482 --> 00:12:10,919
The missing equation comes from conservation of mass.

210
00:12:11,086 --> 00:12:14,711
Water is, for all practical purposes, incompressible.

211
00:12:14,711 --> 00:12:17,898
You can't squeeze a parcel of it into a smaller volume.

212
00:12:17,898 --> 00:12:22,352
So for any small region, whatever flows in must flow out.

213
00:12:22,352 --> 00:12:24,865
Let's count the flow through a small box.

214
00:12:24,865 --> 00:12:29,002
Through the left face, fluid enters at a rate of u at x, times the face's

215
00:12:29,002 --> 00:12:30,565
length, dy.

216
00:12:30,565 --> 00:12:35,936
Through the right face, it leaves at a rate of u at x plus dx, times dy.

217
00:12:35,936 --> 00:12:40,611
The net horizontal outflow is the difference, partial u partial x times dx

218
00:12:40,611 --> 00:12:41,686
dy.

219
00:12:41,686 --> 00:12:46,161
Likewise, the net outflow through the top and bottom is partial v partial

220
00:12:46,161 --> 00:12:48,569
y times dx dy.

221
00:12:48,569 --> 00:12:53,344
The total, per unit volume, is partial u partial x plus partial v partial

222
00:12:53,344 --> 00:12:57,544
y, plus partial w partial z in three dimensions.

223
00:12:57,544 --> 00:13:00,982
This is the divergence of u, written del dot u.

224
00:13:00,982 --> 00:13:05,644
It measures how much fluid is streaming out of each point.

225
00:13:05,794 --> 00:13:08,394
Here's a field with positive divergence:

226
00:13:08,394 --> 00:13:11,544
fluid gushes out of every point, like a source.

227
00:13:11,544 --> 00:13:13,969
Here's negative divergence, a sink.

228
00:13:13,969 --> 00:13:16,494
And here's a field with zero divergence:

229
00:13:16,494 --> 00:13:21,644
fluid swirls and streams around, but around every little region, inflow exactly

230
00:13:21,644 --> 00:13:23,727
balances outflow.

231
00:13:23,727 --> 00:13:28,065
Incompressibility means the divergence is zero everywhere, always.

232
00:13:28,065 --> 00:13:31,052
That's our fourth equation.

233
00:13:31,169 --> 00:13:33,044
And it explains our nozzle.

234
00:13:33,044 --> 00:13:36,957
The flow rate through the pipe must be the same at every cross-section, so

235
00:13:36,957 --> 00:13:39,594
where the area shrinks, the speed must grow.

236
00:13:41,700 --> 00:13:43,300
Here's something curious.

237
00:13:43,300 --> 00:13:47,175
The velocity has an equation telling it how to change in time.

238
00:13:47,175 --> 00:13:48,475
But the pressure doesn't:

239
00:13:48,475 --> 00:13:51,325
there's no partial p partial t anywhere.

240
00:13:51,325 --> 00:13:53,600
So what decides the pressure?

241
00:13:53,600 --> 00:13:54,563
The answer:

242
00:13:54,563 --> 00:13:59,600
pressure is whatever it needs to be to keep the flow incompressible.

243
00:13:59,783 --> 00:14:02,571
Imagine fluid flowing straight toward a wall.

244
00:14:02,571 --> 00:14:08,583
Left alone, with no pressure, it would just pile up against the wall, compressing.

245
00:14:10,283 --> 00:14:14,533
Instead, a region of high pressure forms right in front of the wall.

246
00:14:14,533 --> 00:14:18,946
Its gradient pushes back on the incoming fluid, slowing it down and steering

247
00:14:18,946 --> 00:14:24,283
it aside, exactly enough that nothing ever gets compressed.

248
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:28,638
We can make this precise.

249
00:14:28,638 --> 00:14:31,850
Take the divergence of the whole momentum equation.

250
00:14:31,850 --> 00:14:36,950
The time derivative term vanishes, since the divergence of u is always zero.

251
00:14:36,950 --> 00:14:38,613
So does the viscous term.

252
00:14:38,613 --> 00:14:42,950
What's left is an equation for the pressure alone.

253
00:14:44,358 --> 00:14:49,221
This is a Poisson equation, the same kind of equation that describes gravitational

254
00:14:49,221 --> 00:14:51,108
and electric potentials.

255
00:14:51,108 --> 00:14:55,983
Its solution at any point depends on the flow everywhere else, all at once.

256
00:14:55,983 --> 00:15:01,192
In an incompressible fluid, a push anywhere is felt everywhere instantly.

257
00:15:01,192 --> 00:15:04,379
Physically, the news travels as sound waves.

258
00:15:04,379 --> 00:15:09,092
In water, sound moves at about fifteen hundred meters per second, so fast

259
00:15:09,092 --> 00:15:15,117
compared to everyday flows that 'instantly' is an excellent approximation.

260
00:15:15,258 --> 00:15:18,258
So pressure isn't really an independent player.

261
00:15:18,258 --> 00:15:23,358
It's an enforcer, mathematically a Lagrange multiplier, constantly adjusting

262
00:15:23,358 --> 00:15:28,258
itself so the velocity field never violates the incompressibility constraint.

263
00:15:30,388 --> 00:15:31,713
We have all the pieces.

264
00:15:31,713 --> 00:15:33,472
Let's assemble them.

265
00:15:33,472 --> 00:15:37,809
Density times the acceleration of a parcel, made of the local change plus

266
00:15:37,809 --> 00:15:42,584
the advection term, equals the pressure force, plus the viscous force, plus

267
00:15:42,584 --> 00:15:47,234
any external force per unit volume, like gravity, which would be rho times

268
00:15:47,234 --> 00:15:47,684
g.

269
00:15:47,684 --> 00:15:50,847
And alongside it, incompressibility.

270
00:15:50,847 --> 00:15:54,397
These are the incompressible Navier–Stokes equations.

271
00:15:54,397 --> 00:15:59,584
Claude-Louis Navier first wrote them down in 1822, starting from a model of

272
00:15:59,584 --> 00:16:05,509
forces between molecules, and George Gabriel Stokes rederived them in 1845

273
00:16:05,509 --> 00:16:09,597
from continuum reasoning much like ours.

274
00:16:09,747 --> 00:16:12,984
Often you'll see them divided through by the density.

275
00:16:12,984 --> 00:16:18,297
Then the viscosity divided by the density becomes nu, the kinematic viscosity,

276
00:16:18,297 --> 00:16:23,347
which measures how quickly momentum diffuses through the fluid.

277
00:16:23,547 --> 00:16:25,734
Read the equation like a sentence:

278
00:16:25,734 --> 00:16:30,184
a parcel of fluid, carried along by the flow, changes its velocity because

279
00:16:30,184 --> 00:16:34,334
it's pushed from high pressure to low, because it's dragged toward the velocity

280
00:16:34,334 --> 00:16:37,822
of its neighbors, and because of outside forces.

281
00:16:37,822 --> 00:16:42,122
And through it all, the fluid never compresses.

282
00:16:43,280 --> 00:16:45,855
Four equations, four unknowns.

283
00:16:45,855 --> 00:16:50,355
Add an initial velocity field, and conditions at the boundaries, for instance,

284
00:16:50,355 --> 00:16:55,243
that fluid touching a solid wall sticks to it, the so-called no-slip condition,

285
00:16:55,243 --> 00:16:58,955
and in principle, the entire future of the flow is determined.

286
00:17:01,086 --> 00:17:02,674
So which effect wins?

287
00:17:02,674 --> 00:17:07,111
Inertia, the fluid carrying itself along, or viscosity, smoothing everything

288
00:17:07,111 --> 00:17:07,774
out?

289
00:17:07,774 --> 00:17:10,003
Let's make a rough estimate.

290
00:17:10,003 --> 00:17:14,453
Suppose the flow has a typical speed U, and changes over a typical length

291
00:17:14,453 --> 00:17:18,341
L, like the diameter of a cylinder in a wind tunnel.

292
00:17:18,341 --> 00:17:22,928
The advection term is a velocity times a velocity gradient, so it's roughly

293
00:17:22,928 --> 00:17:25,261
U times U over L.

294
00:17:25,261 --> 00:17:30,736
The viscous term has two derivatives, so it's roughly nu times U over L squared.

295
00:17:30,736 --> 00:17:34,861
Their ratio, U L over nu, is called the Reynolds number.

296
00:17:34,861 --> 00:17:40,086
It's arguably the single most important number in fluid mechanics.

297
00:17:40,253 --> 00:17:42,453
In fact, we can make this exact.

298
00:17:42,453 --> 00:17:47,116
Measure lengths in units of L, velocities in units of U, time in units of

299
00:17:47,116 --> 00:17:51,016
L over U, and pressure in units of rho U squared.

300
00:17:51,016 --> 00:17:56,370
Substitute these into the equation, and divide through by U squared over L.

301
00:17:56,370 --> 00:17:59,545
Everything cancels except a single parameter:

302
00:17:59,545 --> 00:18:03,895
one over the Reynolds number, sitting in front of the viscous term.

303
00:18:03,895 --> 00:18:08,170
Two flows with the same geometry and the same Reynolds number are the same

304
00:18:08,170 --> 00:18:10,520
flow, just rescaled.

305
00:18:10,520 --> 00:18:14,470
That's why engineers can test a small model in a wind tunnel and trust the

306
00:18:14,470 --> 00:18:19,170
results, as long as the Reynolds number matches.

307
00:18:19,345 --> 00:18:23,295
Watch the flow past a cylinder as we dial up the Reynolds number.

308
00:18:23,295 --> 00:18:26,882
At a Reynolds number of one, viscosity dominates.

309
00:18:26,882 --> 00:18:31,132
The flow oozes smoothly around the cylinder, and the disturbance spreads far

310
00:18:31,132 --> 00:18:32,928
out to the sides.

311
00:18:32,928 --> 00:18:37,353
At a Reynolds number of forty, two swirling eddies form behind the cylinder

312
00:18:37,353 --> 00:18:40,386
and just sit there, perfectly steady.

313
00:18:40,386 --> 00:18:44,761
Past roughly fifty, that steady arrangement becomes unstable.

314
00:18:44,761 --> 00:18:48,249
The eddies begin to break away, alternating sides.

315
00:18:48,249 --> 00:18:51,811
The vortex street from the beginning was at a Reynolds number of one hundred

316
00:18:51,811 --> 00:18:53,261
fifty.

317
00:18:53,261 --> 00:18:57,936
Turn it up to a thousand, and the vortices become tighter and more intense,

318
00:18:57,936 --> 00:19:00,524
with thin filaments wrapped around them.

319
00:19:00,524 --> 00:19:05,049
In our two-dimensional simulation the street survives, but in real three-dimensional

320
00:19:05,049 --> 00:19:10,186
flows at this Reynolds number, the wake becomes turbulent.

321
00:19:11,386 --> 00:19:13,724
The range in nature is staggering.

322
00:19:13,724 --> 00:19:17,874
A swimming bacterium lives at a Reynolds number around one ten-thousandth.

323
00:19:17,874 --> 00:19:23,011
For it, water is so syrupy that the moment it stops swimming, it stops dead.

324
00:19:23,011 --> 00:19:26,599
Blood in your aorta flows at Reynolds numbers in the thousands.

325
00:19:26,599 --> 00:19:29,274
A person swimming, around a million.

326
00:19:29,274 --> 00:19:33,011
The air flowing over a jetliner's wing, tens of millions.

327
00:19:34,992 --> 00:19:36,980
Where does all that complexity come from?

328
00:19:36,980 --> 00:19:38,317
Here's a clue.

329
00:19:38,317 --> 00:19:40,567
Start with a simple layered flow:

330
00:19:40,567 --> 00:19:45,117
the middle band moves to the right, and the outer bands move to the left.

331
00:19:45,117 --> 00:19:49,855
Along each interface, tiny ripples get amplified by the nonlinear term, and

332
00:19:49,855 --> 00:19:53,942
the shear layers roll up into chains of spiraling vortices.

333
00:19:53,942 --> 00:19:58,717
This is the Kelvin–Helmholtz instability, the same mechanism that sculpts

334
00:19:58,717 --> 00:20:02,884
those rare clouds shaped like breaking ocean waves.

335
00:20:02,884 --> 00:20:07,147
Then the vortices themselves start to interact, pairing up and merging, and

336
00:20:07,147 --> 00:20:10,384
the orderly picture dissolves.

337
00:20:10,584 --> 00:20:15,009
In a turbulent flow, this kind of thing happens at every scale at once.

338
00:20:15,009 --> 00:20:19,522
Large swirls stretch, fold, and break into smaller swirls, which break into

339
00:20:19,522 --> 00:20:24,172
smaller ones still, until the eddies are so tiny that viscosity finally smooths

340
00:20:24,172 --> 00:20:27,392
them away, turning their energy into heat.

341
00:20:27,392 --> 00:20:31,042
The physicist Lewis Fry Richardson put it as a poem:

342
00:20:31,042 --> 00:20:34,405
Big whorls have little whorls that feed on their velocity;

343
00:20:34,405 --> 00:20:39,867
and little whorls have lesser whorls, and so on to viscosity.

344
00:20:41,051 --> 00:20:43,363
That's the picture in three dimensions.

345
00:20:43,363 --> 00:20:48,276
But two dimensions, which is what all of these simulations are, behaves differently,

346
00:20:48,276 --> 00:20:49,926
in a surprising way.

347
00:20:49,926 --> 00:20:52,788
Watch this two-dimensional turbulent flow.

348
00:20:52,788 --> 00:20:57,026
Instead of shattering into ever-smaller eddies, the swirls tend to merge into

349
00:20:57,026 --> 00:20:59,751
larger and larger vortices.

350
00:20:59,751 --> 00:21:04,051
That difference between two and three dimensions turns out to be at the heart

351
00:21:04,051 --> 00:21:07,176
of the deepest open question about these equations.

352
00:21:14,884 --> 00:21:16,147
Here's the question.

353
00:21:16,147 --> 00:21:20,809
Start with a smooth, well-behaved flow in three dimensions, with finite total

354
00:21:20,809 --> 00:21:21,822
energy.

355
00:21:21,822 --> 00:21:24,484
Let it evolve according to Navier–Stokes.

356
00:21:24,484 --> 00:21:27,784
Does it remain smooth forever?

357
00:21:27,884 --> 00:21:31,784
Or could the velocity somewhere blow up to infinity in a finite amount of

358
00:21:31,784 --> 00:21:37,418
time, a singularity, where the equations themselves stop making sense?

359
00:21:37,418 --> 00:21:39,480
For decades, nobody knew.

360
00:21:39,480 --> 00:21:44,443
In 2000, the Clay Mathematics Institute made this one of its seven Millennium

361
00:21:44,443 --> 00:21:50,168
Prize Problems, offering a million dollars for a proof either way.

362
00:21:50,318 --> 00:21:55,468
To see what's at stake, look at vorticity, omega, the curl of the velocity.

363
00:21:55,468 --> 00:21:59,805
It measures how fast the fluid is spinning locally, like a tiny paddle wheel

364
00:21:59,805 --> 00:22:01,759
dropped into the flow.

365
00:22:01,759 --> 00:22:04,834
Take the curl of the Navier–Stokes equation.

366
00:22:04,834 --> 00:22:09,459
The pressure term disappears, since the curl of a gradient is always zero.

367
00:22:09,459 --> 00:22:14,151
And after some vector calculus, the advection term splits in two.

368
00:22:14,151 --> 00:22:19,251
The result says that vorticity is carried along by the flow, smoothed by viscosity,

369
00:22:19,251 --> 00:22:21,276
and has one extra term:

370
00:22:21,276 --> 00:22:22,564
omega dot del u.

371
00:22:22,564 --> 00:22:25,701
This is vortex stretching.

372
00:22:25,793 --> 00:22:30,155
When a spinning tube of fluid gets stretched along its axis, it gets thinner,

373
00:22:30,155 --> 00:22:34,518
and just like a figure skater pulling in their arms, it spins faster.

374
00:22:34,518 --> 00:22:39,330
Faster spin can drive more stretching, a feedback loop that could, in principle,

375
00:22:39,330 --> 00:22:41,718
run away.

376
00:22:42,943 --> 00:22:46,343
In two dimensions, this term is exactly zero.

377
00:22:46,343 --> 00:22:50,268
The spin axis always points straight out of the plane, and nothing varies

378
00:22:50,268 --> 00:22:53,230
in that direction, so there's nothing to stretch.

379
00:22:53,230 --> 00:22:56,505
That's why the two-dimensional problem was settled long ago:

380
00:22:56,505 --> 00:22:59,676
smooth solutions stay smooth forever.

381
00:22:59,676 --> 00:23:01,951
In three dimensions it's a battle.

382
00:23:01,951 --> 00:23:06,451
Vortex stretching tries to concentrate the spin into ever smaller, ever more

383
00:23:06,451 --> 00:23:07,701
intense regions;

384
00:23:07,701 --> 00:23:10,239
viscosity tries to smooth it all out.

385
00:23:10,239 --> 00:23:13,676
Does viscosity always win?

386
00:23:14,859 --> 00:23:16,047
We do know a lot.

387
00:23:16,047 --> 00:23:22,022
In 1934, Jean Leray proved that weak, generalized solutions always exist.

388
00:23:22,022 --> 00:23:25,459
Smooth solutions are known to exist at least for a while.

389
00:23:25,459 --> 00:23:30,359
And if singularities ever do form, they must be extremely rare, confined to

390
00:23:30,359 --> 00:23:33,484
vanishingly small sets in space and time.

391
00:23:33,484 --> 00:23:37,584
But for nearly a century, whether they can happen at all remained unknown.

392
00:23:40,191 --> 00:23:43,341
Which brings us to a remarkable recent development.

393
00:23:43,341 --> 00:23:49,603
On September 8th, 2026, OpenAI announced that an internal AI system had produced

394
00:23:49,603 --> 00:23:51,691
a proof that the answer is no:

395
00:23:51,691 --> 00:23:54,941
solutions can break down.

396
00:23:56,082 --> 00:23:59,082
There's a subtlety in how the prize problem is phrased.

397
00:23:59,082 --> 00:24:02,832
The official statement, written by Charles Fefferman, offers four ways to

398
00:24:02,832 --> 00:24:03,707
win.

399
00:24:03,707 --> 00:24:07,982
Two ask you to prove that smooth solutions last forever, in all of space or

400
00:24:07,982 --> 00:24:11,507
in a periodic box, with no external force at all.

401
00:24:11,507 --> 00:24:13,532
The other two ask for the opposite:

402
00:24:13,532 --> 00:24:15,657
show that a solution can break down.

403
00:24:15,657 --> 00:24:19,495
And for those, you're allowed to push on the fluid with an external force,

404
00:24:19,495 --> 00:24:23,016
as long as the force itself is perfectly smooth.

405
00:24:23,016 --> 00:24:27,616
That second door, breakdown with a smooth force, is the one OpenAI's result

406
00:24:27,616 --> 00:24:29,991
walks through.

407
00:24:30,149 --> 00:24:32,374
Here is the claim, as announced.

408
00:24:32,374 --> 00:24:37,099
For any viscosity, there is a smooth force, confined to a bounded region,

409
00:24:37,099 --> 00:24:41,499
which, acting on a fluid that starts completely at rest, keeps the total energy

410
00:24:41,499 --> 00:24:46,982
bounded, yet drives the flow to a singularity in a finite amount of time.

411
00:24:46,982 --> 00:24:49,032
Why does the force matter so much?

412
00:24:49,032 --> 00:24:53,032
Without it, you would need a flow that tears itself apart entirely on its

413
00:24:53,032 --> 00:24:53,932
own.

414
00:24:53,932 --> 00:24:57,957
With a force, you get to keep feeding the flow, as long as you never cheat

415
00:24:57,957 --> 00:25:02,632
by using a force that is itself rough, or infinite.

416
00:25:02,757 --> 00:25:07,320
The strategy goes back to Diego Córdoba and Luis Martínez-Zoroa, who had spent

417
00:25:07,320 --> 00:25:10,407
years building what they call an infinite cascade.

418
00:25:10,407 --> 00:25:11,657
Roughly speaking:

419
00:25:11,657 --> 00:25:16,982
an infinite sequence of layers, each one a smaller, faster swirl, each perfectly

420
00:25:16,982 --> 00:25:21,407
smooth on its own, timed so that infinitely many of them pile up in a finite

421
00:25:21,407 --> 00:25:24,899
amount of time, producing a singularity.

422
00:25:24,899 --> 00:25:29,161
Each layer needs its own little push, and the total force is the sum of all

423
00:25:29,161 --> 00:25:30,111
of them.

424
00:25:30,111 --> 00:25:34,936
In their earlier constructions, that infinite sum ended up rough, which falls

425
00:25:34,936 --> 00:25:36,736
short of the prize rules.

426
00:25:36,736 --> 00:25:42,799
The missing step was to build the cascade so that the total force stays smooth.

427
00:25:43,516 --> 00:25:48,566
According to OpenAI, about ten thousand AI agents running on an unreleased

428
00:25:48,566 --> 00:25:53,628
model worked in parallel for about 88 hours to close that gap.

429
00:25:53,628 --> 00:25:58,366
Another model then translated the proof into Lean, a language in which a computer

430
00:25:58,366 --> 00:26:01,416
can check every single logical step.

431
00:26:01,416 --> 00:26:05,691
Charles Fefferman, who wrote the official problem statement, said he was thrilled

432
00:26:05,691 --> 00:26:10,203
that the problem was solved, and credited Córdoba and Martínez-Zoroa as the

433
00:26:10,203 --> 00:26:13,141
heroes of the story.

434
00:26:13,257 --> 00:26:15,257
The story is also tangled.

435
00:26:15,257 --> 00:26:19,582
Just hours before the announcement, mathematicians Tristan Buckmaster and

436
00:26:19,582 --> 00:26:24,482
Levent Alpöge, who had been using AI tools themselves, posted Lean-verified

437
00:26:24,482 --> 00:26:29,032
forced blow-up results for closely related equations, including Euler, the

438
00:26:29,032 --> 00:26:31,320
version with no viscosity.

439
00:26:31,320 --> 00:26:35,607
The two sides dispute how the timelines relate, and OpenAI has said it will

440
00:26:35,607 --> 00:26:38,457
not claim the prize.

441
00:26:38,641 --> 00:26:40,003
So, is it over?

442
00:26:40,003 --> 00:26:41,128
Not quite.

443
00:26:41,128 --> 00:26:45,691
As of this video, the proof is still being checked by human mathematicians,

444
00:26:45,691 --> 00:26:51,066
and the Clay Institute only recognizes a solution after publication and review.

445
00:26:51,066 --> 00:26:55,328
A formal proof is only as good as the check that its formal statement really

446
00:26:55,328 --> 00:26:57,491
says what we think it says.

447
00:26:57,491 --> 00:26:59,466
And notice what it doesn't answer:

448
00:26:59,466 --> 00:27:04,841
whether a fluid left alone, with no force at all, can ever form a singularity.

449
00:27:04,841 --> 00:27:08,766
For the physical question that started all of this, the mystery remains.

450
00:27:10,911 --> 00:27:14,378
Let's step back and look at the equations once more.

451
00:27:14,378 --> 00:27:15,253
On the left:

452
00:27:15,253 --> 00:27:19,778
the acceleration of a fluid parcel, as felt by the parcel itself, carried

453
00:27:19,778 --> 00:27:21,365
along by the flow.

454
00:27:21,365 --> 00:27:22,390
On the right:

455
00:27:22,390 --> 00:27:26,515
pressure pushing from high to low, viscosity smoothing out differences, and

456
00:27:26,515 --> 00:27:28,028
outside forces.

457
00:27:28,028 --> 00:27:31,253
And the promise that the fluid never compresses.

458
00:27:31,253 --> 00:27:34,865
It's just F equals m a, written for a continuum.

459
00:27:34,865 --> 00:27:37,640
Every term has a simple physical meaning.

460
00:27:37,640 --> 00:27:41,878
Yet out of these few pieces comes everything from the rhythm of a vortex street,

461
00:27:41,878 --> 00:27:45,428
to the chaos of turbulence, to the weather on our planet.

462
00:27:45,428 --> 00:27:50,353
And whether a fluid, left entirely to itself, can ever tear itself apart is

463
00:27:50,353 --> 00:27:53,261
still waiting for someone to figure out.

464
00:27:53,261 --> 00:27:54,436
Thanks for watching.
